        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink mb-3">Getting Started with OnSciCalc</h2>
        <p>
          Using OnSciCalc is simple. Visit the website and start typing your mathematical expression. You can use the on-screen buttons or your physical keyboard. Press <strong>Enter</strong> to evaluate, <strong>Backspace</strong> to delete the last character, and <strong>Escape</strong> to clear everything.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The calculator supports two modes: <strong>Beginner</strong> for basic operations and <strong>Advanced</strong> for complex scientific functions. Switch between <strong>DEG</strong> (degrees) and <strong>RAD</strong> (radians) for trigonometric calculations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink mb-3">Understanding Operator Precedence</h2>
        <p>
          Every scientific calculator follows a standard order of operations. OnSciCalc evaluates expressions in this order:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline p-4 rounded-md font-geist-mono text-xs space-y-1 mt-3">
          <div>1. Parentheses ( )</div>
          <div>2. Exponents x^y</div>
          <div>3. Functions sin, cos, tan, log, ln</div>
          <div>4. Multiplication and Division *, /</div>
          <div>5. Addition and Subtraction +, -</div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Example: <code class="bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-brand-link font-geist-mono text-xs">2 + 3 * 4</code> = 14 (multiplication first), but <code class="bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-brand-link font-geist-mono text-xs">(2 + 3) * 4</code> = 20 (parentheses first).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink mb-3">Working with Trigonometric Functions</h2>
        <p>
          OnSciCalc supports sin, cos, tan and their inverse functions. The calculator also supports implicit multiplication — <code class="bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-brand-link font-geist-mono text-xs">3(4+2)</code> = 18.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink mb-3">Logarithms and Exponents</h2>
        <p>
          Use <strong>log</strong> for base-10 logarithms and <strong>ln</strong> for natural logarithms. The ^ operator handles exponents: <code class="bg-canvas-soft-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-brand-link font-geist-mono text-xs">2^3</code> = 8.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
